@module dart-m06 | Object-Oriented Programming
@summary Model your domain with classes, constructors, inheritance, interfaces, mixins, extensions, generics and enums.

@lesson dart-m06-l01 | Classes | 14
## Learning objectives
- Define classes with fields, getters and methods
- Override `toString`, `==` and `hashCode`
- Follow Dart naming and design conventions

## A blueprint for objects
A **class** describes the data (fields) and behaviour (methods) of a kind of object.

```dart
class BankAccount {
  BankAccount(this.owner, [this._balanceCents = 0]);

  final String owner;          // field (final: set once)
  int _balanceCents;           // private field

  int get balanceCents => _balanceCents;          // getter
  bool get isOverdrawn => _balanceCents < 0;      // computed getter

  void deposit(int cents) {                       // method
    if (cents <= 0) throw ArgumentError.value(cents, 'cents', 'must be positive');
    _balanceCents += cents;
  }

  @override
  String toString() => 'BankAccount($owner, $_balanceCents¢)';
}

void main() {
  final account = BankAccount('Ada');
  account.deposit(2500);
  print(account);               // BankAccount(Ada, 2500¢)
  print(account.balanceCents);  // 2500
}
```

Class names use **UpperCamelCase**; fields and methods use **lowerCamelCase**.

## Fields, getters and setters
Fields store state. A **getter** (`get`) computes a value on demand and is called without parentheses. A **setter** (`set`) runs code when a value is assigned:

```dart
class Temperature {
  Temperature(this.celsius);
  double celsius;
  double get fahrenheit => celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;
  set fahrenheit(double f) => celsius = (f - 32) * 5 / 9;
}
```

## Equality
By default `==` compares **identity**: two objects are equal only if they are the same instance. For value-like classes, override `==` and `hashCode` together:

```dart
class Money {
  const Money(this.cents, this.currency);
  final int cents;
  final String currency;

  @override
  bool operator ==(Object other) =>
      other is Money && other.cents == cents && other.currency == currency;

  @override
  int get hashCode => Object.hash(cents, currency);
}

void main() {
  print(const Money(100, 'EUR') == const Money(100, 'EUR')); // true
  print({const Money(1, 'EUR'), const Money(1, 'EUR')}.length); // 1
}
```

> IMPORTANT: Whenever you override `==`, also override `hashCode`, otherwise sets and maps behave incorrectly.

Writing this by hand gets tedious; packages like `equatable` or code generators like `freezed` produce it for you, and Dart records (Module 7) have value equality built in.

:::check Why must hashCode be overridden together with ==?
Hash-based collections (Set, Map) first compare hash codes; equal objects must have equal hash codes or they will be treated as different.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Making every field public and mutable. Prefer `final` fields and expose behaviour through methods.
- Overriding `==` without `hashCode`.

## Summary
- Classes bundle fields, getters, setters and methods.
- Getters compute values; setters validate or convert assignments.
- Override `toString` for readable output and `==`/`hashCode` together for value equality.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Classes
