@lesson dart-m06-l02 | Objects and instances | 10
## Learning objectives
- Create instances and access their members
- Understand references, identity and object lifetime
- Use `this`, cascades and null-aware member access with objects

## Creating instances
Calling a class's constructor creates an **instance** (an object). The `new` keyword is optional and omitted in modern Dart.

```dart
class Lesson {
  Lesson(this.title, {this.minutes = 10});
  final String title;
  final int minutes;
  bool completed = false;

  void complete() => completed = true;
}

void main() {
  final a = Lesson('Variables');
  final b = Lesson('Functions', minutes: 15);
  a.complete();
  print('${a.title}: ${a.completed}'); // Variables: true
  print('${b.title}: ${b.completed}'); // Functions: false
}
```

Each instance has its **own** copy of the instance fields. Completing `a` does not affect `b`.

## Variables hold references
A variable doesn't contain the object itself; it contains a **reference** to it. Assigning one variable to another copies the reference:

```dart
class Lesson {
  Lesson(this.title);
  final String title;
  bool completed = false;
  void complete() => completed = true;
}

void main() {
  final first = Lesson('Loops');
  final alias = first;
  alias.complete();
  print(first.completed);          // true – same object
  print(identical(first, alias));  // true
}
```

## this
Inside a class, `this` refers to the current instance. You usually omit it, but it's needed when a parameter shadows a field:

```dart
class Counter {
  int value = 0;
  void setValue(int value) {
    this.value = value; // field = parameter
  }
}
```

## Cascades on objects
```dart
final counter = Counter()
  ..value = 5
  ..setValue(10);
print(counter.value); // 10
```

## Lifetime and garbage collection
Dart automatically frees objects that are no longer reachable from any variable. You don't delete objects manually, but you *do* need to release external resources (streams, controllers, sockets) explicitly, typically in a `dispose` or `close` method.

> TIP: In Flutter, objects like `TextEditingController` and `AnimationController` must be disposed in `State.dispose()`; garbage collection alone does not stop their listeners.

:::check After `final b = a;`, are a and b two objects?
No. They are two variables referencing the same object.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Expecting assignment to copy an object. Create a copy explicitly (for example with a `copyWith` method).
- Forgetting to dispose resource-holding objects.

## Summary
- Constructors create instances; each instance has its own fields.
- Variables hold references; assignment shares the same object.
- `this` refers to the current instance; cascades configure objects fluently.
- Memory is garbage-collected, but external resources need explicit disposal.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Classes ▸ Using class members
