@lesson dart-m06-l03 | Constructors | 14
## Learning objectives
- Write generative constructors with initialising formals
- Use initializer lists and assertions
- Create const constructors and redirecting constructors

## Generative constructors
The most common constructor has the same name as the class. **Initialising formals** (`this.field`) assign parameters directly to fields:

```dart
class User {
  User(this.name, this.email, {this.isAdmin = false});
  final String name;
  final String email;
  final bool isAdmin;
}
```

This is shorthand for writing the parameters and assigning them, and it works with `final` fields because the assignment happens *before* the constructor body runs.

## Initializer lists
Use an **initializer list** (after `:`) to compute fields from parameters or validate them with `assert`:

```dart
class Rectangle {
  Rectangle(double width, double height)
      : assert(width >= 0 && height >= 0, 'Sides must be non-negative'),
        area = width * height,
        _width = width,
        _height = height;

  final double area;
  final double _width;
  final double _height;

  double get perimeter => 2 * (_width + _height);
}

void main() {
  final r = Rectangle(3, 4);
  print('${r.area} ${r.perimeter}'); // 12.0 14.0
}
```

`assert` checks run in debug mode (and in tests); they're stripped from release builds. For checks that must always run, throw an `ArgumentError` in the constructor body or a factory.

## Super parameters
When extending a class, pass values to the parent with `super.` parameters (Dart 2.17+):

```dart
class Animal {
  Animal(this.name);
  final String name;
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  Dog(super.name, {required this.breed});
  final String breed;
}
```

## Const constructors
If all fields are `final` and the constructor has no body, mark it `const`. Then instances can be compile-time constants and are canonicalised:

```dart
class Point {
  const Point(this.x, this.y);
  final int x;
  final int y;
}

void main() {
  const a = Point(1, 2);
  const b = Point(1, 2);
  print(identical(a, b)); // true
}
```

This is why almost every Flutter widget has a `const` constructor.

## Redirecting constructors
A constructor can delegate to another one with `: this(...)`:

```dart
class Color {
  const Color(this.red, this.green, this.blue);
  const Color.grey(int level) : this(level, level, level);
  final int red, green, blue;
}
```

> NOTE: If you declare no constructor, Dart provides a default unnamed constructor with no parameters.

:::check When does an assert in an initializer list run?
Only when assertions are enabled: in debug mode and tests, not in release builds.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Trying to assign `final` fields in the constructor body; use initialising formals or the initializer list.
- Relying on `assert` for validation that must happen in production.

## Summary
- `ClassName(this.field)` assigns parameters to fields concisely.
- Initializer lists compute fields and run asserts before the body.
- `super.param` forwards values to the superclass constructor.
- `const` constructors enable compile-time constants; redirecting constructors reuse logic.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Constructors
