@lesson dart-m06-l04 | Named constructors | 10
## Learning objectives
- Declare named constructors for alternative ways to create objects
- Write `fromJson` style constructors
- Combine named constructors with redirection and const

## Multiple ways to build an object
Dart doesn't support overloading constructors by parameter types. Instead you give extra constructors **names**: `ClassName.name(...)`.

```dart
class Duration2 {
  const Duration2(this.seconds);
  const Duration2.minutes(int m) : seconds = m * 60;
  const Duration2.hours(int h) : seconds = h * 3600;
  const Duration2.zero() : seconds = 0;
  final int seconds;
}

void main() {
  print(const Duration2.minutes(5).seconds); // 300
  print(const Duration2.hours(1).seconds);   // 3600
}
```

The Dart SDK uses this pattern everywhere: `DateTime.now()`, `DateTime.utc(...)`, `List.filled(...)`, `List.generate(...)`, `Uri.parse(...)`.

## fromJson
The most common named constructor in app code converts decoded JSON into a model:

```dart
class Course {
  Course({required this.id, required this.title, this.lessons = 0});

  Course.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json)
      : id = json['id'] as String,
        title = json['title'] as String,
        lessons = (json['lessons'] as int?) ?? 0;

  final String id;
  final String title;
  final int lessons;

  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() => {'id': id, 'title': title, 'lessons': lessons};
}

void main() {
  final course = Course.fromJson({'id': 'dart', 'title': 'Complete Dart'});
  print('${course.title} has ${course.lessons} lessons');
  print(course.toJson());
}
```

## Named constructors and inheritance
Constructors are **not inherited**. A subclass that wants `fromJson` must declare its own and call the appropriate superclass constructor.

## Naming
Choose names that describe *how* the object is made or *what* it represents: `Point.origin()`, `Color.fromHex()`, `User.guest()`, `Rect.fromLTWH()`.

> TIP: Use a named constructor when the alternative way of creating the object is obvious from its name. When creation needs logic like caching or choosing a subtype, use a factory constructor (next lesson).

:::check Can Dart have two unnamed constructors with different parameter types?
No. Dart has no constructor overloading; use named constructors instead.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Expecting a subclass to inherit its parent's named constructors.
- Using unchecked casts in `fromJson` on untrusted data; validate or use patterns for robustness.

## Summary
- Named constructors (`Class.name()`) provide alternative creation paths.
- They're ideal for `fromJson`, unit conversions and special values.
- Constructors are not inherited.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Constructors ▸ Named constructors
