@lesson dart-m06-l05 | Factory constructors | 12
## Learning objectives
- Write factory constructors that can return existing or subtype instances
- Implement caching and validation with factories
- Know the difference between factories and static methods

## Constructors that don't have to create
A normal (generative) constructor always creates a new instance of exactly its class. A **factory** constructor is a constructor whose body decides what to return: a new instance, a cached one, or an instance of a subtype.

```dart
class Logger {
  factory Logger(String name) => _cache.putIfAbsent(name, () => Logger._internal(name));
  Logger._internal(this.name);

  final String name;
  static final _cache = <String, Logger>{};

  void log(String message) => print('[$name] $message');
}

void main() {
  final a = Logger('network');
  final b = Logger('network');
  print(identical(a, b)); // true – cached
  a.log('connected');
}
```

`Logger._internal` is a private named constructor that only the factory uses.

## Returning a subtype
```dart
abstract class Shape {
  factory Shape(String type, double size) => switch (type) {
        'circle' => Circle(size),
        'square' => Square(size),
        _ => throw ArgumentError('Unknown shape: $type'),
      };
  double get area;
}

class Circle implements Shape {
  Circle(this.radius);
  final double radius;
  @override
  double get area => 3.14159 * radius * radius;
}

class Square implements Shape {
  Square(this.side);
  final double side;
  @override
  double get area => side * side;
}

void main() {
  final s = Shape('square', 3);
  print('${s.runtimeType} ${s.area}'); // Square 9.0
}
```

## Validation
Factories can validate input and throw in production (unlike `assert`):

```dart
class Email {
  factory Email(String value) {
    final trimmed = value.trim();
    if (!trimmed.contains('@')) throw FormatException('Invalid email', value);
    return Email._(trimmed.toLowerCase());
  }
  const Email._(this.value);
  final String value;
}
```

## fromJson as a factory
Many codebases write `factory User.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) => User(...)`, which lets the body run arbitrary logic before calling the real constructor.

## Factory vs static method
Both can return instances. A factory is called like a constructor (`Logger('x')`), can be `const`-redirecting, and keeps the "constructor" API shape. A static method (`Logger.create('x')`) can be `async` and can return `null` or a `Future`. Factories cannot be async.

> NOTE: A factory has no access to `this`; it must return an object explicitly.

:::check Can a factory constructor return a cached instance?
Yes. That is one of its main uses.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Trying to use `this` inside a factory.
- Making a factory async; use a static method returning a `Future` instead.

## Summary
- Factory constructors control what instance is returned.
- Use them for caching, returning subtypes and validation.
- Private named constructors (`_internal`) often back a public factory.
- Use static methods when you need async creation.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Constructors ▸ Factory constructors
