@lesson dart-m06-l07 | Encapsulation | 10
## Learning objectives
- Hide implementation details with library-private members
- Expose behaviour through methods and read-only getters
- Protect invariants and avoid leaking mutable state

## Hiding details
**Encapsulation** means keeping an object's internal state private and exposing a small, safe API. In Dart, privacy is **per library**: names starting with `_` are visible only inside the file that declares them.

```dart
class ShoppingCart {
  final List<String> _items = [];

  List<String> get items => List.unmodifiable(_items);
  int get count => _items.length;

  void add(String item) {
    if (item.trim().isEmpty) return;
    _items.add(item.trim());
  }

  void remove(String item) => _items.remove(item);
}

void main() {
  final cart = ShoppingCart()
    ..add('Book')
    ..add('  ');
  print(cart.count); // 1 – blank item rejected
  // cart.items.add('Hack'); // throws UnsupportedError: list is unmodifiable
}
```

The cart guarantees it never contains blank items (an **invariant**). Because outside code can't touch `_items` directly, the invariant can't be broken.

## Don't leak mutable internals
Returning `_items` directly would let callers modify it, bypassing your rules. Return an unmodifiable view or a copy.

## Read-only properties
A public `final` field or a getter without a setter is read-only from the outside:

```dart
class Account {
  Account(this.id);
  final String id;          // read-only
  int _balance = 0;
  int get balance => _balance;  // read-only view of private state
}
```

## Library privacy in practice
Because privacy is per file, you can keep helper classes private next to the class that uses them. Large apps rely on this together with a `src/` folder convention: files under `lib/src/` are considered implementation details of a package.

> TIP: Start with everything private and make members public only when another file needs them. It is easy to widen an API later and hard to narrow it.

:::check Can code in another file read a field named `_balance`?
No. Identifiers starting with an underscore are private to their library (file).
:::

## Common mistakes
- Exposing a mutable list via a public getter.
- Writing trivial getters and setters for every private field (Java style); a public field is fine until you need logic.

## Summary
- Encapsulation hides state and exposes behaviour.
- `_` makes members library-private.
- Protect invariants with validation in methods; return unmodifiable views or copies.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Effective Dart ▸ Design ▸ Members
@quiz
? tf: In Dart, a member named `_secret` is private to its class.
= false
> Underscore privacy is per library (file), not per class.
? mcq: What is the safest way to expose a private `_items` list?
+ Return List.unmodifiable(_items) from a getter
- Make _items public
- Return _items directly
- Make _items dynamic
> An unmodifiable view prevents callers from bypassing your rules.
? fill: A rule that must always hold for an object, like "no blank items", is called an ___.
= invariant
> Encapsulation protects invariants.
? scenario: Teammates keep setting `user.age = -5` directly. What change enforces valid ages?
+ Make the field private and expose a method or setter that validates the value
- Add a comment saying "don't do this"
- Make age dynamic
- Rename the field
> Encapsulation lets you validate changes in one place.
? mcq: Effective Dart's advice on getters and setters is:
+ Use a public field until you need logic, then switch to a getter/setter
- Always write getX and setX methods
- Never use getters
- Make every field static
> Dart getters/setters can replace fields later without changing callers.

@lesson dart-m06-l08 | Polymorphism | 12
## Learning objectives
- Use subtype polymorphism to write general code
- Understand dynamic dispatch
- Replace type checks with overridden methods or sealed types

## One interface, many forms
**Polymorphism** lets code work with a general type while each object supplies its own behaviour:

```dart
abstract class Notification {
  String get title;
  String render();
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
  EmailNotification(this.subject);
  final String subject;
  @override
  String get title => 'Email';
  @override
  String render() => '📧 $subject';
}

class PushNotification implements Notification {
  PushNotification(this.text);
  final String text;
  @override
  String get title => 'Push';
  @override
  String render() => '🔔 $text';
}

void showAll(List<Notification> notifications) {
  for (final n in notifications) {
    print('${n.title}: ${n.render()}'); // calls the right render()
  }
}

void main() {
  showAll([EmailNotification('Welcome'), PushNotification('New lesson unlocked')]);
}
```

`showAll` doesn't know or care about concrete types. Adding a new `SmsNotification` requires **no changes** to `showAll`.

## Dynamic dispatch
At runtime, `n.render()` calls the implementation of the **actual object's** class, not the declared type. This is called dynamic dispatch, and it's what makes polymorphism work.

## Replacing type switches
Code full of `if (x is A) ... else if (x is B) ...` is often a sign that behaviour belongs in the classes themselves:

```dart
// Fragile: every new type needs another branch here
double areaOf(Object s) {
  if (s is Circle2) return 3.14 * s.r * s.r;
  if (s is Square2) return s.side * s.side;
  throw ArgumentError();
}

class Circle2 { Circle2(this.r); final double r; }
class Square2 { Square2(this.side); final double side; }
```

Better: give each shape an `area` getter. When you *do* need an external switch (for example, rendering a UI per state), use a **sealed class** (Module 7) so the compiler checks you've handled every subtype.

## Polymorphism in Flutter
Every widget is a `Widget`. A `Column` holds `List<Widget>` children and calls `build`/`createElement` on each without knowing whether it's a `Text`, an `Image` or your own `LessonCard`.

> TIP: Program to an interface (abstract type), not an implementation. It keeps code flexible and testable: in tests you can pass a fake implementation.

:::check What determines which render() runs: the variable's declared type or the object's runtime type?
The object's runtime type (dynamic dispatch).
:::

## Common mistakes
- Long chains of `is` checks instead of polymorphic methods.
- Depending on concrete classes everywhere, making testing hard.

## Summary
- Polymorphism lets one piece of code work with many types.
- Dynamic dispatch selects the implementation of the runtime type.
- Prefer overridden methods (or sealed types with exhaustive switches) over manual type checks.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Classes ▸ Implicit interfaces
@quiz
? output: What does this print?
```dart
class Animal {
  String sound() => '...';
}

class Cat extends Animal {
  @override
  String sound() => 'meow';
}

void main() {
  Animal a = Cat();
  print(a.sound());
}
```
+ meow
- ...
- A compile error
- null
> Dynamic dispatch calls the runtime type's override.
? tf: Adding a new Notification subtype requires changing a function that only calls `notification.render()`.
= false
> Polymorphic code works with new subtypes automatically.
? mcq: What is a common sign that polymorphism could improve your code?
+ Long chains of `if (x is SomeType)` checks
- Using final fields
- Having getters
- Using const constructors
> Type-check chains often mean behaviour belongs in the classes.
? fill: Choosing the implementation based on the object's runtime type is called dynamic ___.
= dispatch
> Dynamic dispatch makes polymorphism work.
? scenario: You want unit tests to use a fake payment gateway instead of the real one. Which design enables this?
+ Depend on an abstract PaymentGateway type and pass in an implementation
- Hard-code the real gateway class everywhere
- Use static methods only
- Copy the gateway code into tests
> Programming to an interface allows substituting fakes.

@lesson dart-m06-l09 | Abstract classes | 10
## Learning objectives
- Declare abstract classes and abstract members
- Mix abstract and concrete members
- Use abstract classes to define contracts

## Classes you can't instantiate
An **abstract class** can't be instantiated directly. It may declare **abstract members** (no body) that subclasses must implement, alongside concrete members they inherit:

```dart
abstract class Exporter {
  String get fileExtension;              // abstract getter
  String encode(Map<String, Object> data); // abstract method

  String fileName(String base) => '$base.$fileExtension'; // concrete, shared
}

class JsonExporter extends Exporter {
  @override
  String get fileExtension => 'json';

  @override
  String encode(Map<String, Object> data) =>
      '{${data.entries.map((e) => '"${e.key}": "${e.value}"').join(', ')}}';
}

class CsvExporter extends Exporter {
  @override
  String get fileExtension => 'csv';

  @override
  String encode(Map<String, Object> data) =>
      '${data.keys.join(',')}\n${data.values.join(',')}';
}

void main() {
  final exporters = <Exporter>[JsonExporter(), CsvExporter()];
  for (final e in exporters) {
    print(e.fileName('progress'));
    print(e.encode({'xp': 120, 'level': 3}));
  }
  // Exporter(); // Error: abstract classes can't be instantiated
}
```

## Why abstract classes?
- **Contracts**: they define what implementations must provide.
- **Shared code**: concrete members (like `fileName`) are written once.
- **Template method pattern**: a concrete method calls abstract steps that subclasses fill in.

## Abstract classes as dependencies
Repositories, services and data sources in clean architecture are typically declared as abstract classes:

```dart
abstract class LessonRepository {
  Future<List<String>> lessonTitles();
}

class FakeLessonRepository implements LessonRepository {
  @override
  Future<List<String>> lessonTitles() async => ['Variables', 'Loops'];
}
```

The rest of the app depends on `LessonRepository`; tests use `FakeLessonRepository`.

## Abstract classes vs interfaces vs mixins
- Use an **abstract class** when you want a contract plus shared implementation.
- Use an **interface** (any class used with `implements`, or an `interface class`) when you only want the contract.
- Use a **mixin** to share behaviour across unrelated classes.

> NOTE: In Dart 3 you can write `abstract interface class` to declare a pure contract that can only be implemented, not extended.

:::check Can an abstract class have methods with bodies?
Yes. Abstract classes can mix abstract and concrete members.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Trying to instantiate an abstract class.
- Forgetting to implement an abstract member: the subclass must also be abstract or implement everything.

## Summary
- Abstract classes cannot be instantiated and may declare members without bodies.
- Subclasses implement abstract members and inherit concrete ones.
- They're ideal for contracts with shared behaviour and for dependency abstractions.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Classes ▸ Abstract classes
@quiz
? tf: You can create an instance of an abstract class with `Shape()` if it has a default constructor.
= false
> Abstract classes cannot be instantiated (only factories can return other instances).
? mcq: What must a concrete subclass of an abstract class do?
+ Implement all abstract members
- Override every method, including concrete ones
- Declare a const constructor
- Be private
> Abstract members have no implementation and must be provided.
? fill: A method declared without a body in an abstract class is called an ___ method.
= abstract
> Abstract methods only define the signature.
? scenario: Several exporters share the same `fileName` logic but encode data differently. What structure fits?
+ An abstract class with a concrete fileName and an abstract encode
- Copy fileName into each exporter
- A single class with a big switch
- A global function per exporter
> Abstract classes combine shared code and required overrides.
? mcq: Which Dart 3 declaration creates a pure contract that cannot be extended, only implemented?
+ abstract interface class Repo {}
- sealed mixin Repo {}
- final abstract Repo {}
- interface Repo {}
> abstract interface class declares an implement-only contract.

@lesson dart-m06-l10 | Interfaces | 12
## Learning objectives
- Use any class as an interface with `implements`
- Implement multiple interfaces
- Use Dart 3 `interface class` and `abstract interface class`

## Implicit interfaces
Every class in Dart **implicitly defines an interface** made of its public members. Another class can promise to provide those members with `implements`, without inheriting any code:

```dart
class Printer {
  void printDocument(String doc) => print('Printing $doc');
}

class Scanner {
  String scan() => 'scanned page';
}

class OfficeMachine implements Printer, Scanner {
  @override
  void printDocument(String doc) => print('Office printing: $doc');

  @override
  String scan() => 'high-resolution scan';
}

void main() {
  final machine = OfficeMachine();
  Printer p = machine;
  Scanner s = machine;
  p.printDocument('report.pdf');
  print(s.scan());
}
```

Key differences from `extends`:
- `implements` inherits **no implementation**: you must write every member.
- A class can implement **many** interfaces but extend only one class.

## Pure contracts with abstract interface class
Dart 3's class modifiers let you state intent explicitly:

```dart
abstract interface class Cache {
  String? read(String key);
  void write(String key, String value);
}

class MemoryCache implements Cache {
  final _data = <String, String>{};
  @override
  String? read(String key) => _data[key];
  @override
  void write(String key, String value) => _data[key] = value;
}
```

`abstract interface class` can't be instantiated or extended outside its library; it can only be implemented. This is the clearest way to declare a dependency contract.

## Implementing for testing
Because any class is an interface, you can fake even concrete classes in tests:

```dart
class Clock {
  DateTime now() => DateTime.now();
}

class FixedClock implements Clock {
  FixedClock(this.fixed);
  final DateTime fixed;
  @override
  DateTime now() => fixed;
}
```

Bimo's own Android code uses this idea for its time provider, so streak logic can be tested with fixed dates.

> TIP: Use `implements` to describe *capabilities* ("can be cached", "can be compared") and `extends` for genuine "is-a" relationships with shared code.

:::check Does implementing an interface give you the interface's method bodies?
No. With implements you must provide every member yourself.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Using `implements` and then wondering why you must re-implement every member; use `extends` if you want inherited code.
- Forgetting that implementing a class also requires implementing its getters and fields (as getters/setters).

## Summary
- Every class defines an implicit interface.
- `implements` promises members without inheriting implementation; multiple interfaces are allowed.
- `abstract interface class` declares an explicit, implement-only contract.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Classes ▸ Implicit interfaces
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Class modifiers
@quiz
? tf: A Dart class can implement several interfaces.
= true
> implements accepts a comma-separated list of types.
? mcq: What does `class B implements A` inherit from A's methods?
- Their implementations
+ Nothing; B must implement all of A's members
- Only private methods
- Only static methods
> implements only adopts the interface, not the code.
? fill: To use class A as an interface, write `class B ___ A`.
= implements
> implements declares that B satisfies A's interface.
? scenario: Tests must control the current time used by streak calculations. What is the cleanest approach?
+ Depend on a Clock abstraction and pass a FixedClock implementation in tests
- Change the device clock during tests
- Call DateTime.now() directly and hope
- Use a global mutable variable
> An interface allows substituting a deterministic implementation.
? mcq: Which declaration can only be implemented, not instantiated or extended (outside its library)?
+ abstract interface class Cache
- class Cache
- mixin Cache
- enum Cache
> abstract interface class is an implement-only contract.

@lesson dart-m06-l11 | Mixins | 12
## Learning objectives
- Share behaviour across unrelated classes with mixins
- Restrict mixins with `on`
- Understand mixin application order

## Reusing code without inheritance
A **mixin** is a bundle of members that can be added to a class with `with`. It's Dart's answer to "I want this behaviour in several classes that don't share a parent".

```dart
mixin Loggable {
  final _log = <String>[];
  void log(String message) => _log.add('${DateTime.now().millisecondsSinceEpoch}: $message');
  List<String> get history => List.unmodifiable(_log);
}

mixin Timestamped {
  final createdAt = DateTime.now();
}

class Order with Loggable, Timestamped {
  Order(this.id) {
    log('Order $id created');
  }
  final String id;
}

class UserSession with Loggable {
  void signIn() => log('Signed in');
}

void main() {
  final order = Order('A-1');
  final session = UserSession()..signIn();
  print(order.history.length);   // 1
  print(session.history.length); // 1
  print(order is Loggable);      // true
}
```

`Order` and `UserSession` are unrelated, yet both gain logging.

## Restricting with on
A mixin can require that the class it's applied to extends a certain type, so it can call that type's members:

```dart
abstract class Widget2 {
  void markNeedsRepaint() => print('repaint');
}

mixin Blinking on Widget2 {
  void blink() {
    markNeedsRepaint(); // allowed: we know we're on a Widget2
  }
}

class Cursor extends Widget2 with Blinking {}
```

## Order matters
Mixins are applied left to right; later mixins override earlier ones, and `super` calls go to the previous one in the chain:

```dart
mixin A {
  String hello() => 'A';
}
mixin B {
  String hello() => 'B';
}
class C with A, B {}

void main() => print(C().hello()); // B
```

## Mixins in Flutter
You'll meet mixins like `SingleTickerProviderStateMixin` (for animations) and `WidgetsBindingObserver` (for app lifecycle events):

```dart
// class _MyAnimationState extends State<MyAnimation>
//     with SingleTickerProviderStateMixin { ... }
```

> NOTE: A mixin can't have a generative constructor. Since Dart 3, a class can only be used as a mixin if it's declared with `mixin` or `mixin class`.

:::check In `class C with A, B`, which mixin's method wins if both define hello()?
B, because mixins are applied left to right and later ones override earlier ones.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Using mixins to share *state-heavy* behaviour that would be clearer as a separate object (composition).
- Forgetting `on` when the mixin calls members of a specific superclass.

## Summary
- `mixin` declares reusable members; `with` applies them.
- `on` restricts which classes can use a mixin.
- Later mixins override earlier ones.
- Flutter uses mixins for tickers and lifecycle observation.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Mixins
