```dart
mixin X {
  String name() => 'X';
}
mixin Y {
  String name() => 'Y';
}
class Z with Y, X {}

void main() {
  print(Z().name());
}
```
+ X
- Y
- XY
- A compile error
> Mixins apply left to right; X is applied last and wins.
? fill: Mixins are added to a class with the keyword ___.
= with
> class Order with Loggable applies the mixin.
? tf: A mixin can declare a generative constructor.
= false
> Mixins cannot have generative constructors.
? mcq: What does `mixin Blinking on Widget2` mean?
+ Blinking can only be applied to classes that extend Widget2
- Blinking extends Widget2 automatically
- Widget2 must use Blinking
- Blinking is abstract
> on restricts the mixin's superclass so it can use Widget2 members.
? scenario: An Order and a UserSession, which share no superclass, both need identical logging methods. What's the most idiomatic tool?
+ A Loggable mixin applied to both classes
- Make Order extend UserSession
- Copy-paste the methods
- A sealed class
> Mixins share behaviour across unrelated classes.

@lesson dart-m06-l12 | Extension methods | 10
## Learning objectives
- Add methods and getters to existing types with extensions
- Understand static resolution of extensions
- Use extension types for zero-cost wrappers

## Adding methods to types you don't own
You can't edit `String` or `DateTime`, but you can **extend** them with new members:

```dart
extension StringX on String {
  String get capitalized => isEmpty ? this : '${this[0].toUpperCase()}${substring(1)}';
  bool get isValidEmail => RegExp(r'^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$').hasMatch(this);
  String truncate(int max) => length <= max ? this : '${substring(0, max)}…';
}

extension DurationFormat on Duration {
  String get mmss {
    final m = inMinutes.remainder(60).toString().padLeft(2, '0');
    final s = inSeconds.remainder(60).toString().padLeft(2, '0');
    return '$m:$s';
  }
}

void main() {
  print('flutter'.capitalized);               // Flutter
  print('ada@example.com'.isValidEmail);      // true
  print('A very long lesson title'.truncate(10)); // A very lon…
  print(const Duration(seconds: 125).mmss);   // 02:05
}
```

Inside the extension, `this` is the value the member was called on.

## Extensions on generic and nullable types
```dart
extension NullableStringX on String? {
  bool get isNullOrBlank => this == null || this!.trim().isEmpty;
}

extension ListX<T> on List<T> {
  T? get secondOrNull => length > 1 ? this[1] : null;
}
```

## Static resolution
Extensions are resolved using the **static type** of the expression, not the runtime type. They don't work on `dynamic` values, and they can't override existing members: if `String` already has a member with the same name, the class member wins.

## Importing
Extensions are available wherever their library is imported. If two extensions conflict, hide one or call it explicitly: `StringX('x').capitalized`.

## Extension types (Dart 3.3)
An **extension type** wraps an existing type with a new, compile-time-only interface. It costs nothing at runtime:

```dart
extension type UserId(String value) {
  bool get isGuest => value.startsWith('guest-');
}

void main() {
  final id = UserId('guest-42');
  print(id.isGuest); // true
  // String s = id;  // Error: UserId is not a String
}
```

This prevents mixing up, say, a `UserId` and a `LessonId` that are both strings underneath.

> TIP: Keep extensions small and focused. A `string_extensions.dart` file with dozens of unrelated helpers becomes hard to discover.

:::check Can an extension method be called on a variable of type dynamic?
No. Extensions are resolved statically, so dynamic receivers don't see them.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Expecting an extension to override an existing method of the class.
- Overusing extensions for business logic that belongs in a proper class.

## Summary
- `extension Name on Type { ... }` adds methods and getters to existing types.
- Resolution is static; class members take precedence.
- Extension types (Dart 3.3+) create zero-cost, type-safe wrappers.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Extension methods
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Extension types
@quiz
? output: What does this print?
```dart
extension on int {
  int get doubled => this * 2;
}

void main() {
  print(21.doubled);
}
```
+ 42
- 21
- 2121
- A compile error
> The extension getter multiplies the receiver by 2.
? tf: Extension methods can override existing methods of the extended class.
= false
> If the class already has the member, the class member wins.
? fill: Inside an extension, the receiver value is referred to as ___.
= this
> this is the value the extension member was called on.
? mcq: Why don't extensions work on `dynamic` values?
+ Extensions are resolved using the static type at compile time
- dynamic values are always null
- Extensions only work on classes you own
- The runtime removes extensions
> Static resolution needs a known static type.
? scenario: UserId and LessonId are both Strings, and developers keep passing one where the other is expected. What Dart 3 feature prevents this at zero runtime cost?
+ Extension types such as `extension type UserId(String value)`
- Mixins
- Factory constructors
- dynamic
> Extension types give distinct static types without wrapper objects.

@lesson dart-m06-l13 | Generics | 14
## Learning objectives
- Write generic classes, functions and methods
- Constrain type parameters with `extends`
- Understand reified generics and type safety

## Why generics?
Generics let you write code that works with **many types** while staying type-safe. `List<String>` is a list that only holds strings; you wrote the list type once, and it works for any element type.

```dart
class Box<T> {
  Box(this.value);
  T value;
  Box<R> map<R>(R Function(T) transform) => Box(transform(value));
}

void main() {
  final age = Box<int>(36);
  final label = age.map((v) => 'Age: $v'); // Box<String>
  print(label.value); // Age: 36
  // age.value = 'old'; // Error: a String isn't an int
}
```

`T` is a **type parameter**: a placeholder filled in when the class is used.

## Generic functions
```dart
T firstOr<T>(List<T> items, T fallback) => items.isEmpty ? fallback : items.first;

void main() {
  print(firstOr(<int>[], 0));          // 0
  print(firstOr(['a', 'b'], 'none'));  // a – T inferred as String
}
```

## Bounded type parameters
Use `extends` to require that the type supports certain operations:

```dart
T maxOf<T extends Comparable<T>>(List<T> items) {
  var best = items.first;
  for (final item in items.skip(1)) {
    if (item.compareTo(best) > 0) best = item;
  }
  return best;
}

void main() {
  print(maxOf(['pear', 'apple', 'fig']));                    // pear
  print(maxOf([DateTime(2024, 5, 1), DateTime(2025, 1, 9)])); // 2025-01-09 00:00:00.000
}
```

Without the bound, `compareTo` wouldn't be available on `T`.

> NOTE: `int` implements `Comparable<num>`, not `Comparable<int>`, so `maxOf([3, 9, 4])` can't infer `T` with this bound. A common workaround is the bound `T extends Comparable<Object>`, or simply using `reduce(max)` from `dart:math` for numbers.

## A practical generic: Result
Generic result types make success and failure explicit:

```dart
sealed class Result<T> {
  const Result();
}

class Ok<T> extends Result<T> {
  const Ok(this.value);
  final T value;
}

class Err<T> extends Result<T> {
  const Err(this.message);
  final String message;
}

Result<int> parseAge(String input) {
  final n = int.tryParse(input);
  return n == null ? Err('Not a number: $input') : Ok(n);
}

void main() {
  final result = parseAge('42');
  final text = switch (result) {
    Ok(:final value) => 'Age $value',
    Err(:final message) => 'Error: $message',
  };
  print(text);
}
```

## Reified generics
Dart keeps type arguments at runtime (they are **reified**), so `items is List<int>` really checks the element type. This is unlike Java, where generic types are erased.

> TIP: Name type parameters with single capital letters by convention: `T` (type), `E` (element), `K`/`V` (key/value), `R` (result).

:::check What does `<T extends num>` allow you to do inside the function?
Call num members, such as arithmetic operators or `toDouble()`, on values of type T.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Using `dynamic` instead of a type parameter, losing type safety.
- Forgetting a bound and then being unable to call needed methods on `T`.

## Summary
- Generics parameterise classes and functions by type.
- Bounds (`T extends X`) constrain what types are allowed and unlock members.
- Dart generics are reified and checked at runtime.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Generics
@quiz
? output: What does this print?
```dart
T pick<T>(T a, T b, bool first) => first ? a : b;

void main() {
  print(pick('x', 'y', false));
}
```
+ y
- x
- false
- A compile error
> first is false, so b is returned.
? fill: To require that T supports compareTo, declare `<T ___ Comparable<T>>`.
= extends
> Bounded type parameters use extends.
? tf: Dart erases generic type arguments at runtime like Java does.
= false
> Dart generics are reified; type arguments exist at runtime.
? output: What does this print?
```dart
void main() {
  final List<Object> items = <int>[1, 2];
  print(items is List<int>);
}
```
+ true
- false
- A compile error
- null
> The runtime type argument int is preserved and checked.
? mcq: Which conventional type parameter name is used for collection elements?
+ E
- X
- Q
- Z
> E stands for element, as in List<E>.
? scenario: A function currently takes and returns `dynamic` so it works for any type, but bugs slip through. What's the better signature?
+ A generic function such as `T identity<T>(T value)`
- Object everywhere with casts
- void return type
- Separate functions for every type
> Generics keep type safety while staying reusable.

@lesson dart-m06-l14 | Enums | 12
## Learning objectives
- Declare simple and enhanced enums
- Add fields, constructors, getters and methods to enums
- Use enums in exhaustive switches and serialisation

## A fixed set of values
An **enum** defines a type with a fixed set of named values:

```dart
enum Difficulty { easy, medium, hard }

void main() {
  const d = Difficulty.medium;
  print(d);                 // Difficulty.medium
  print(d.name);            // medium
  print(d.index);           // 1
  print(Difficulty.values); // [Difficulty.easy, Difficulty.medium, Difficulty.hard]
  print(Difficulty.values.byName('hard')); // Difficulty.hard
}
```

Enums replace "magic strings" like `'medium'`, which are easy to misspell and impossible for the compiler to check.

## Enhanced enums
Since Dart 2.17, enums can have fields, constructors, getters and methods, as long as everything is `final`/`const`:

```dart
enum Plan {
  free(priceCents: 0, adFree: false),
  monthly(priceCents: 799, adFree: true),
  yearly(priceCents: 4999, adFree: true);

  const Plan({required this.priceCents, required this.adFree});

  final int priceCents;
  final bool adFree;

  String get price => priceCents == 0 ? 'Free' : '\$${(priceCents / 100).toStringAsFixed(2)}';
  bool get isPaid => priceCents > 0;
}

void main() {
  for (final plan in Plan.values) {
    print('${plan.name}: ${plan.price}, ad-free: ${plan.adFree}');
  }
}
```

Bimo's premium plans and achievement tiers are modelled exactly like this.

## Exhaustive switches
Because the set of values is fixed, switches over enums are checked for exhaustiveness:

```dart
enum TrafficLight { red, amber, green }

String action(TrafficLight light) => switch (light) {
  TrafficLight.red => 'Stop',
  TrafficLight.amber => 'Get ready',
  TrafficLight.green => 'Go',
};
```

Add `TrafficLight.flashing` and every non-exhaustive switch becomes a compile error, guiding you to all the places that need updating.

## Implementing interfaces
Enums can implement interfaces and use mixins:

```dart
enum Size implements Comparable<Size> {
  small(1), medium(2), large(3);
  const Size(this.rank);
  final int rank;
  @override
  int compareTo(Size other) => rank - other.rank;
}
```

## Persisting enums
Store `enum.name` (a stable string) in databases or JSON and read it back with `values.byName`. Avoid storing `index`: reordering values would silently corrupt saved data.

> WARNING: `values.byName` throws if the name isn't found. When reading untrusted data, use `Plan.values.where((p) => p.name == raw).firstOrNull` and handle null.

:::check Why is storing `enum.index` in a database risky?
If someone reorders or inserts enum values, the stored numbers map to different values.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Using strings or ints instead of enums for fixed sets of options.
- Adding a `default`/`_` case to enum switches, which disables exhaustiveness checks.

## Summary
- Enums define fixed sets of values with `name`, `index` and `values`.
- Enhanced enums add const fields, constructors, getters and methods.
- Enum switches are exhaustive; persist enums by `name`.

